    "growth_rate_source": "FY2026 guidance (low-single-digit service revenue growth through 2028; H1-26 revenue +2.6% YoY), not a historical CAGR",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Every 5-year CAGR is divestiture-distorted: FY2020-21 include WarnerMedia (spun April 2022) and DIRECTV, so revenue (-2.6%), equity (-6.7%) and owner FCF (-7.6%) describe a different company. EPS CAGRs are undefined (FY2020 and FY2022 GAAP losses from impairments). The 3-year equity CAGR (5.9%) is inflated by FY2025 gains on the DIRECTV and tower sales. Guidance-based 2.5% is used instead.",
    "notes": "Owner FCF = reported FCF (OCF - capex) - SBC; interest income not deducted (net debt). FY2022 OCF/FCF is missing from the CSV, so the 3-year FCF CAGR is null; FY2023 owner FCF $19,982M -> FY2025 $18,906M is -2.7%/yr, the result of capex stepping up for fiber, not of SBC (SBC flat at ~$0.5B)."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Management's multi-year framework roughly delivered: low-single-digit service revenue growth, adjusted EBITDA +3.5% in 2026 accelerating to ~5% by 2028 as legacy copper is decommissioned and fiber penetration scales toward 60M passings, then a plateau at a 38.5% margin. Capital investment stays at ~$23-24B through the build, then network capex fades while a recurring spectrum charge (~1.5% of revenue) replaces the EchoStar-type lumps. Cash taxes rise from the OBBBA-depressed ~3% to a normal 22%. Wireless share holds near the improved Q2-26 run-rate (432K postpaid phone adds, 0.86% churn) without T-Mobile or Verizon starting a price war.",
    "bull": "Convergence proves a structural margin lever: 42-45% bundle attach compounds as fiber reaches 60M+ passings, churn stays sub-0.9%, EchoStar spectrum lets AT&T take share at lower incremental capex, and the Business Wireline copper drag disappears faster than guided once California-style carrier-of-last-resort obligations fall away. Revenue grows ~3.5% mid-decade, EBITDA margin reaches 40%, network capex settles near 14.5% with JV partners (Gigapower, Forged Fiber 37) carrying part of the build, and spectrum needs stay modest.",
    "bear": "Wireless reverts to a promotional share fight (T-Mobile momentum, Verizon-Frontier convergence, Boost riding AT&T's own network as a hybrid MNO), legacy Business Wireline goes EBITDA-negative after 2027 as management itself warned and copper retirement is slowed by state carrier-of-last-resort disputes, and Starlink direct-to-cell plus fixed wireless erode rural fiber returns. Revenue grows ~1%, margin stalls near 36%, and capex never comes down because spectrum (an expensive upper C-band auction) and capacity spend replace fiber spend. Leverage near 3x keeps the refinancing bill high but solvency is not in question."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Owner-FCF DCF on an unlevered basis at a 7% USD WACC, with every cost an owner actually bears charged once: SBC in the flows, vendor-financing payments inside capital investment, a normalised spectrum charge for the purchases AT&T's own FCF definition leaves out, the EchoStar cash in the bridge, preferred-like NCI and postemployment obligations deducted at book. The ~$18B 'FCF' headline is levered and excludes spectrum; it is not the owner's cash.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Terminal value is capped at 12-15x terminal FCF; at a 7% WACC Gordon would be ~20x, so the cap is doing real work and the value is sensitive to it.",
      "Spectrum normalisation (1-2.5pp of revenue) is judgment; each 1pp of revenue is ~$1.3B/yr, roughly $2.5-3/share.",
      "Equity is ~half of EV; each 1% change in EV moves equity value ~2%.",
      "Cash-tax ramp: if 100% bonus depreciation persists and capex stays above D&A, the 22% terminal cash rate is conservative.",
      "Debt figure: Metrics.csv TotalDebt double-counts the current portion; this build uses XBRL book debt (~$134.6B) - using the CSV figure would understate value by ~$1.35/share."
    ]
  },
